<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lhasa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lhasa is the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, at an altitude of about 3,650 metres in the valley of the Kyi River. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> made it the capital of the Tibetan Empire in the 7th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> temple founded in his time became the holiest shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. From 1642 it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>' government, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> overlooks the city, and since 1951 it has been under Chinese rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lhasa.jpg" alt="Lhasa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pavel Špindler · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_-_Pot%C3%A1la_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1717 his commander Tsering Dondub crossed the Tibetan plateau, killed the Khoshut ruler Lha-bzang Khan and sacked <b>Lhasa</b>; a Qing relief army was destroyed in 1718, but a second expedition expelled the Dzungars in 1720.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade between India and Tibet passed through Kathmandu, Newar merchants kept houses in <b>Lhasa</b>, and Nepal minted silver coins that circulated in Tibet until disputes over debased coinage helped spark war in 1788.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The peace treaty Tibet swore with Tang China in 821–822 was carved in Tibetan and Chinese on a stone pillar that still stands in front of the Jokhang temple in <b>Lhasa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its monuments include the stupa of Sanchi, the painted caves of Ajanta, the colossal Buddhas of Bamiyan, destroyed by the Taliban in 2001, the temple-mountain of Borobudur, the pagodas of Bagan and the temples of Kyoto and <b>Lhasa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1642 the Oirat ruler Güshi Khan conferred authority over Tibet on the Fifth Dalai Lama, whose Ganden Phodrang government ruled from <b>Lhasa</b> under Mongol and, from 1720, Qing protection.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>An uprising began in <b>Lhasa</b> after two Qing ambans assassinated Gyurme Namgyal, the expected new regent of Tibet; both ambans were killed, with 51 Qing soldiers and 77 Chinese citizens.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-11">11 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
